module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}

// Which of a chunk's names may live in numbered cells, which is a question asked NAME BY NAME.
//
// **The rule is that NOTHING OUTSIDE THE CHUNK MAY BE ABLE TO REACH A NAME.** Two things can, and
// both reach a name by its SPELLING rather than by holding the chunk:
//
// - **A nested function**, which captures the scope chain by pointer and reads a name out of it
//   whenever it is called -- possibly long after the frame that declared the name has returned. So
//   every FREE NAME of a nested `Lambda` or `Def` is one this chunk keeps in the chain. A `class`
//   and a `data` declaration are covered by that already, both desugaring to an object of lambdas.
// - **A pattern that outlives the block**, which is what a `type` declaration makes: `PushShape`
//   captures the scope so that a `ProtoPat` inside the pattern can find the class it names, and the
//   shape is a value that can be passed anywhere. So the names such a pattern carries are kept too.
//
// **IT WAS A QUESTION ABOUT THE WHOLE BODY AND ASKING IT PER NAME IS WHAT REACHES ORDINARY CODE.**
// One `Lambda` anywhere used to cost a chunk every cell it had, and most functions hold a lambda
// somewhere -- so the analysis that could be checked by reading was also the one that gave up on
// nearly everything real. What replaces it is the same walk carrying a third list: the names a
// nested body can reach, gathered exhaustively, and every other name of the chunk is still a cell.
//
// **THE ANSWER IS TWO FLAGS AND A LIST, because a chunk can now be half of each.** `whole` says the
// chunk needs no `EnvObj` at all -- nothing of its own is reachable, so its parameters are cells and
// no block pushes a scope. `cells` says only that SOME name may take one. A chunk holding a closure
// that reads one of its names is `cells` and not `whole`: that one name stays in the scope the
// closure captured, and the rest move into the frame.
//
// **EVERY BINDING FORM IS FOLLOWED NOW, AND THE ONES THAT BIND THROUGH A PATTERN SHARE ONE
// MECHANISM.** A `for` head, a `match` arm and a destructuring `val` all go through the matcher, and
// the matcher gathers what it bound onto the operand stack where the compiler claimed cells for it;
// a `catch` binds one name and no pattern and is a plain `declare_name`. What each contributes here
// is the same two things: the names it binds join this chunk's own, and the names its pattern looks
// a TYPE up by join the ones a slotted name may not collide with.
//
// **A pattern whose two walks could disagree is what is refused instead**, which is `slottable_pattern`
// -- a default, a `?`, and the handful of kinds `pattern_names` gathers nothing from that could
// still bind. The chunk keeps its scope and nothing else changes.
//
// **A PARAMETER'S DEFAULT IS NO LONGER ONE OF THEM.** It was, while the question "was this argument
// given" could only be asked of the scope; the frame carries a bitmask now and `JumpIfGiven` asks it
// directly. What is still asked of a default is what is asked of the body -- what a closure in it
// can reach -- because `f(g = () -> x)` puts a closure in the head of the chunk as surely as the
// body could.
//
// **A CHUNK THAT IS NOT `whole` KEEPS ALL ITS PARAMETERS IN THE SCOPE, and that is a decision rather
// than a limitation of the walk.** Parameter `i` is slot `i` by construction, which is what lets a
// call lay its arguments down with no table travelling from the compiler to the machine; a chunk
// where some parameters were cells and some were not would need that table back. So the parameters
// move together, and what per-name slotting buys such a chunk is its LOCALS.
//
// **A chunk with more parameters than the mask has bits does not slot.** Nothing real has 64, and a
// silent wrap would make `JumpIfGiven` answer about the wrong parameter.
//
// **AND IT REFUSES A CHUNK WHOSE `is` BINDS A NAME, which is the one binding form left in a chunk
// that otherwise slots.** `v is n` puts `n` into the scope the match is running in -- and a slotted
// chunk's scope is the one its closure CAPTURED, so the binding outlives the call and every later
// call sees it. `pattern_binds` is the question, and it is asked of an annotation and a result
// promise too: both are patterns and a bare name in one is a binding wherever no type of that name
// is declared.
//
// **AND A NAME A PATTERN IN THE BODY TESTS AGAINST IS KEPT, for the same reason a captured one is.**
// A class registers its name as a type whose pattern is `ProtoPat(name)`, and that pattern looks the
// name up **in the scope the match is running in** -- so `f(A) = a is A`, where the parameter shadows
// the class, is deliberately false. A name in a cell is not in that scope at all, so the lookup would
// walk past the shadow and find the class, and the answer would quietly become true.
//
// It is asked of every name a pattern carries rather than only of the ones that turn out to be
// types, because which names are types is not known here -- and keeping a name that would have been
// fine costs speed, where celling one that is not costs an answer.
//
// Not `private`: `compile.sysl` asks it of each chunk before emitting the body.
slot_plan(params: Buf[string], defaults: Buf[Expr], body: Expr) -> Plan
    if params.len() > MaskBits then return Plan(false, false, buf())

    // **The names this chunk binds, and the names something outside it can reach by spelling**, both
    // filled by the one walk below rather than by two more of their own -- every arm that would
    // collect them is an arm it already has.
    var mine: Buf[string] = buf()
    var kept: Buf[string] = buf()

    for i in 0..<params.len()
        mine.push(params.at(i))

    for i in 0..<defaults.len()
        if expr_holds_capture(defaults.at(i), &mine, &kept) then return Plan(false, false, buf())

    if expr_holds_capture(body, &mine, &kept) then return Plan(false, false, buf())

    // **A chunk NEEDS its scope the moment one of its own names is kept**, and only then. Everything
    // else about it is unchanged: the kept name is declared and read by name exactly as it always
    // was, and every other name of the chunk is a cell.
    for i in 0..<kept.len()
        for j in 0..<mine.len()
            if kept.at(i) == mine.at(j) then return Plan(true, false, kept)

    Plan(true, true, kept)
end slot_plan

// What a chunk's names may do. **Two answers rather than one**, which is the whole of what per-name
// slotting changed: they were the same question while a chunk either celled everything or nothing.
struct Plan
    // Whether ANY name declared here may take a cell.
    cells: bool

    // Whether the chunk needs no `EnvObj` at all, which is the stronger answer and implies the other.
    whole: bool

    // The names something outside the chunk can reach by spelling, which keep `LoadName`.
    kept: Buf[string]
end Plan

// How many parameters a frame's mask can speak for, which is the width of the machine's own word.
const MaskBits: usize = 64

private expr_holds_capture(x: Expr, mine: *Buf[string], kept: *Buf[string]) -> bool
    *x.kind match
        // **A closure reads its free names out of the scope it captured**, so those are the names
        // this chunk keeps -- and the ones it does not name are still cells, which is the whole of
        // what this step buys. The lambda's own parameters shadow for the length of its body, so a
        // `(x) -> x * 2` written beside an `x` of ours reaches nothing.
        Lambda(params, defaults, body, _) ->
            reachable_from(params, defaults, body, kept)
            false

        // **A shape is a VALUE and can be matched anywhere**, long after this frame has gone, and
        // `PushShape` captures the scope for a `ProtoPat` inside it to look a class up in. So every
        // name its pattern carries is kept, exactly as a name a `match` arm tests against is.
        ShapeLit(name, pat) ->
            note_mention(name, kept)
            pattern_mentions(pat, kept)
            pattern_binders(pat, kept)
            false

        // **The three forms that bind through a PATTERN.** Each is followed rather than refused
        // now: the names its pattern binds join this chunk's own, and the names its pattern looks a
        // type up by join the ones a slotted name may not collide with.
        MatchExpr(subject, arms) ->
            for i in 0..<arms.len()
                val arm = arms.at(i)

                if !slottable_pattern(arm.pattern, false) then return true

                pattern_binders(arm.pattern, mine)
                pattern_mentions(arm.pattern, kept)

                if maybe_expr(arm.guard, mine, kept) then return true

                if expr_holds_capture(arm.body, mine, kept) then return true

            expr_holds_capture(subject, mine, kept)
        For(_, binding, iter, body, otherwise, _) ->
            if !bindable_here(binding, mine, kept) then return true

            expr_holds_capture(iter, mine, kept) || expr_holds_capture(body, mine, kept) || maybe_expr(otherwise, mine, kept)

        // **A `catch` binds ONE name and no pattern**, which is what makes it the binding form that
        // can be taught first: `compile_try` emits the binding itself rather than handing a pattern
        // to the matcher, so it is a `declare_name` and nothing else. The name joins this chunk's
        // own, so a pattern in the body may not also mean it.
        TryCatch(body, name, handler) ->
            mine.push(name)
            expr_holds_capture(body, mine, kept) || expr_holds_capture(handler, mine, kept)

        Spread(inner) -> expr_holds_capture(inner, mine, kept)
        Named(_, value) -> expr_holds_capture(value, mine, kept)
        Unary(_, rhs) -> expr_holds_capture(rhs, mine, kept)
        Binary(_, lhs, rhs) -> expr_holds_capture(lhs, mine, kept) || expr_holds_capture(rhs, mine, kept)
        Call(callee, args) -> expr_holds_capture(callee, mine, kept) || any_expr(args, mine, kept)
        Index(target, key) -> expr_holds_capture(target, mine, kept) || expr_holds_capture(key, mine, kept)
        Field(target, _, _) -> expr_holds_capture(target, mine, kept)
        ArrayLit(items) -> any_expr(items, mine, kept)
        ArrayRepeat(value, count) -> expr_holds_capture(value, mine, kept) || expr_holds_capture(count, mine, kept)
        Interp(pieces) -> any_expr(pieces, mine, kept)
        WithFields(base, _, values) -> expr_holds_capture(base, mine, kept) || any_expr(values, mine, kept)
        WithAll(base, other, _) -> expr_holds_capture(base, mine, kept) || expr_holds_capture(other, mine, kept)
        ObjectLit(_, _, values) -> any_expr(values, mine, kept)
        DataLit(inner) -> expr_holds_capture(inner, mine, kept)
        Compare(operands, _) -> any_expr(operands, mine, kept)
        Range(lo, hi, _) -> maybe_expr(lo, mine, kept) || maybe_expr(hi, mine, kept)
        IsPattern(subject, pat, _) ->
            if pattern_binds(pat) then return true

            pattern_mentions(pat, kept)
            expr_holds_capture(subject, mine, kept)
        IncDec(target, _, _) -> expr_holds_capture(target, mine, kept)
        Await(inner) -> expr_holds_capture(inner, mine, kept)
        Yield(inner) -> expr_holds_capture(inner, mine, kept)
        IfExpr(cond, then_branch, else_branch) ->
            expr_holds_capture(cond, mine, kept) || expr_holds_capture(then_branch, mine, kept) || maybe_expr(else_branch, mine, kept)
        While(_, cond, body, otherwise) ->
            expr_holds_capture(cond, mine, kept) || expr_holds_capture(body, mine, kept) || maybe_expr(otherwise, mine, kept)
        Loop(_, body) -> expr_holds_capture(body, mine, kept)
        DoWhile(_, body, cond, otherwise) ->
            expr_holds_capture(body, mine, kept) || expr_holds_capture(cond, mine, kept) || maybe_expr(otherwise, mine, kept)
        Block(stmts) -> any_stmt(stmts, mine, kept)
        Ensures(value, _, pat) ->
            if pattern_binds(pat) then return true

            pattern_mentions(pat, kept)
            expr_holds_capture(value, mine, kept)

        // Every literal and every bare name. **Written out rather than left to a wildcard on
        // purpose**: a node added to the tree should make this stop compiling and be classified,
        // because the cost of a new node quietly falling into "holds nothing" is a name in a slot
        // that something outside the chunk can still reach.
        IntLit(_) -> false
        RealLit(_) -> false
        StrLit(_) -> false
        BoolLit(_) -> false
        NullLit -> false
        UndefinedLit -> false
        Name(_) -> false

        // A node the parser put down where it could not read one. Nothing is emitted for it, but a
        // chunk that failed to parse is not one to reason about either.
        Bad -> true
end expr_holds_capture

private stmt_holds_capture(s: Stmt, mine: *Buf[string], kept: *Buf[string]) -> bool
    *s.kind match
        // **A definition is a closure and its own NAME is written into the scope by `DefineFn`**, so
        // the name is kept along with everything its body can reach. It reaches itself, recursion
        // being the ordinary case, and the walk below would gather that anyway.
        Def(name, params, defaults, body, _) ->
            note_mention(name, kept)
            reachable_from(params, defaults, body, kept)
            false

        // A `type` interns its pattern into a shape and declares the name by hand, which is
        // `ShapeLit` with a binding after it.
        TypeDecl(name, pat) ->
            note_mention(name, kept)
            pattern_mentions(pat, kept)
            pattern_binders(pat, kept)
            false

        // The two forms that take a slot, so their names are the ones a pattern must not also mean.
        Val(name, init) ->
            mine.push(name)
            expr_holds_capture(init, mine, kept)
        Var(name, init) ->
            mine.push(name)
            expr_holds_capture(init, mine, kept)
        Unpack(pat, init, _) ->
            if !bindable_here(pat, mine, kept) then return true

            expr_holds_capture(init, mine, kept)
        Group(stmts) -> any_stmt(stmts, mine, kept)
        Assign(_, target, value) -> expr_holds_capture(target, mine, kept) || expr_holds_capture(value, mine, kept)
        MultiAssign(_, targets, values) -> any_expr(targets, mine, kept) || any_expr(values, mine, kept)
        Eval(e) -> expr_holds_capture(e, mine, kept)
        Return(value) -> maybe_expr(value, mine, kept)
        Throw(value) -> expr_holds_capture(value, mine, kept)
        Break(_, value) -> maybe_expr(value, mine, kept)
        Continue(_) -> false

        // An import binds names the module it names owns, and cannot appear inside a function
        // anyway. Written out for the reason the literals above are.
        ImportNames(_, _, _, _, _) -> true
        ImportAll(_, _, _, _) -> true
        ImportAsset(_, _, _) -> true

        // A parameter's annotation, which the parser puts at the top of the body. Its pattern names a
        // type, and a type is a name looked up where the match runs.
        CheckType(_, _, pat) ->
            if pattern_binds(pat) then return true

            pattern_mentions(pat, kept)
            false
end stmt_holds_capture

private any_expr(xs: Buf[Expr], mine: *Buf[string], kept: *Buf[string]) -> bool
    for i in 0..<xs.len()
        if expr_holds_capture(xs.at(i), mine, kept) then return true

    false
end any_expr

private any_stmt(xs: Buf[Stmt], mine: *Buf[string], kept: *Buf[string]) -> bool
    for i in 0..<xs.len()
        if stmt_holds_capture(xs.at(i), mine, kept) then return true

    false
end any_stmt

private maybe_expr(x: Option[Expr], mine: *Buf[string], kept: *Buf[string]) -> bool = x match
    Some(e) -> expr_holds_capture(e, mine, kept)
    None -> false

// -- what a nested body can reach --------------------------------------------------------------------

// The names a nested function can reach through the scope chain it captured.
//
// **Its own parameters are taken back out, and nothing else is.** A parameter list scopes the whole
// body and every default in it, so a lambda writing `x` beside an enclosing `x` reaches its own and
// the enclosing one is still a cell. A `val` inside the nested body shadows too, but telling that
// apart wants a walk that carries scopes -- and keeping a name the nested body only ever declared
// for itself costs this chunk one cell, where getting it the other way round costs an answer.
private reachable_from(params: Buf[string], defaults: Buf[Expr], body: usize, into: *Buf[string])
    var inner: Buf[string] = buf()

    for i in 0..<defaults.len()
        free_names(defaults.at(i), &inner)

    free_names(body_at(body), &inner)

    for i in 0..<inner.len()
        val name = inner.at(i)
        var shadowed = false

        for j in 0..<params.len()
            if plain_param(params.at(j)) == name then shadowed = true

        if !shadowed then note_mention(name, into)
end reachable_from

// Every name a body mentions, wherever it mentions one.
//
// **It gathers what a name COULD mean rather than what it does**, which is why a `val` the body
// declares for itself is in the answer alongside a name it read from outside. Deciding which is
// which needs the scopes, and the conservative direction here is the safe one: an extra name costs
// the enclosing chunk a cell, and a missing one costs it a wrong answer.
//
// **Exhaustive over `ExprKind` on purpose and it must stay so**, for the reason
// `expr_holds_capture` above it is: a node added later that quietly falls into "mentions nothing"
// is a name in a cell that a closure can still reach, which nothing would report.
private free_names(x: Expr, into: *Buf[string])
    *x.kind match
        Name(n) -> note_mention(n, into)
        Lambda(params, defaults, body, _) -> reachable_from(params, defaults, body, into)
        ShapeLit(name, pat) ->
            note_mention(name, into)
            pattern_reach(pat, into)
        MatchExpr(subject, arms) ->
            free_names(subject, into)

            for i in 0..<arms.len()
                val arm = arms.at(i)

                pattern_reach(arm.pattern, into)
                maybe_free(arm.guard, into)
                free_names(arm.body, into)
        For(_, binding, iter, body, otherwise, _) ->
            pattern_reach(binding, into)
            free_names(iter, into)
            free_names(body, into)
            maybe_free(otherwise, into)
        TryCatch(body, name, handler) ->
            note_mention(name, into)
            free_names(body, into)
            free_names(handler, into)
        Spread(inner) -> free_names(inner, into)
        Named(_, value) -> free_names(value, into)
        Unary(_, rhs) -> free_names(rhs, into)
        Binary(_, lhs, rhs) ->
            free_names(lhs, into)
            free_names(rhs, into)
        Call(callee, args) ->
            free_names(callee, into)
            each_free(args, into)
        Index(target, key) ->
            free_names(target, into)
            free_names(key, into)

        // **A FIELD'S NAME IS NOT A NAME IN ANY SCOPE**, so only what it is read off is walked. The
        // same goes for an object literal's keys and a `with`'s, which is why those arms ignore them.
        Field(target, _, _) -> free_names(target, into)
        ArrayLit(items) -> each_free(items, into)
        ArrayRepeat(value, count) ->
            free_names(value, into)
            free_names(count, into)
        Interp(pieces) -> each_free(pieces, into)
        WithFields(base, _, values) ->
            free_names(base, into)
            each_free(values, into)
        WithAll(base, other, _) ->
            free_names(base, into)
            free_names(other, into)
        ObjectLit(_, _, values) -> each_free(values, into)
        DataLit(inner) -> free_names(inner, into)
        Compare(operands, _) -> each_free(operands, into)
        Range(lo, hi, _) ->
            maybe_free(lo, into)
            maybe_free(hi, into)
        IsPattern(subject, pat, _) ->
            free_names(subject, into)
            pattern_reach(pat, into)
        IncDec(target, _, _) -> free_names(target, into)
        Await(inner) -> free_names(inner, into)
        Yield(inner) -> free_names(inner, into)
        IfExpr(cond, then_branch, else_branch) ->
            free_names(cond, into)
            free_names(then_branch, into)
            maybe_free(else_branch, into)
        While(_, cond, body, otherwise) ->
            free_names(cond, into)
            free_names(body, into)
            maybe_free(otherwise, into)
        Loop(_, body) -> free_names(body, into)
        DoWhile(_, body, cond, otherwise) ->
            free_names(body, into)
            free_names(cond, into)
            maybe_free(otherwise, into)
        Block(stmts) -> each_free_stmt(stmts, into)
        Ensures(value, _, pat) ->
            free_names(value, into)
            pattern_reach(pat, into)

        // Every literal. Written out rather than left to a wildcard for the reason the walk above
        // writes its own out.
        IntLit(_) -> ()
        RealLit(_) -> ()
        StrLit(_) -> ()
        BoolLit(_) -> ()
        NullLit -> ()
        UndefinedLit -> ()

        // A node the parser put down where it could not read one. There are no names to gather off
        // it, and the chunk holding it will not compile -- `expr_holds_capture` refuses one outright
        // and a nested body that failed to parse takes the whole file's report with it.
        Bad -> ()
end free_names

private free_stmt(s: Stmt, into: *Buf[string])
    *s.kind match
        Def(name, params, defaults, body, _) ->
            note_mention(name, into)
            reachable_from(params, defaults, body, into)
        TypeDecl(name, pat) ->
            note_mention(name, into)
            pattern_reach(pat, into)
        Val(name, init) ->
            note_mention(name, into)
            free_names(init, into)
        Var(name, init) ->
            note_mention(name, into)
            free_names(init, into)
        Unpack(pat, init, _) ->
            pattern_reach(pat, into)
            free_names(init, into)
        Group(stmts) -> each_free_stmt(stmts, into)
        Assign(_, target, value) ->
            free_names(target, into)
            free_names(value, into)
        MultiAssign(_, targets, values) ->
            each_free(targets, into)
            each_free(values, into)
        Eval(e) -> free_names(e, into)
        Return(value) -> maybe_free(value, into)
        Throw(value) -> free_names(value, into)
        Break(_, value) -> maybe_free(value, into)
        Continue(_) -> ()

        // An import binds the names it lands under, which a closure below it reads like any other.
        ImportNames(_, _, _, _, locals) ->
            for i in 0..<locals.len()
                note_mention(locals.at(i), into)
        ImportAll(_, _, _, alias) -> note_mention(alias, into)
        ImportAsset(_, _, name) -> note_mention(name, into)

        // A parameter's annotation, which the parser puts at the top of the body: the name it checks
        // and whatever type its pattern looks up.
        CheckType(name, _, pat) ->
            note_mention(name, into)
            pattern_reach(pat, into)
end free_stmt

// Every name a pattern carries, bound or looked up. **Both lists, because a nested body reaching one
// of them is the same event either way** -- what matters here is the spelling and not what it means.
private pattern_reach(pat: Pattern, into: *Buf[string])
    pattern_mentions(pat, into)
    pattern_binders(pat, into)

private each_free(xs: Buf[Expr], into: *Buf[string])
    for i in 0..<xs.len()
        free_names(xs.at(i), into)

private each_free_stmt(xs: Buf[Stmt], into: *Buf[string])
    for i in 0..<xs.len()
        free_stmt(xs.at(i), into)

private maybe_free(x: Option[Expr], into: *Buf[string]) = x match
    Some(e) -> free_names(e, into)
    None -> ()

// -- emitting a name, one way or the other ----------------------------------------------------------

// **These three are the ONLY places that decide between a slot and the chain**, which is what keeps
// the two from disagreeing: a name is claimed here and looked up here, so a declaration and every
// read of it read the same table.

// Bind a name in the block being emitted.
//
// **The question is about THIS NAME and not about the chunk**, which is what a chunk holding a
// closure buys: the one name that closure reads is declared in the scope exactly as it always was,
// and every other name of the chunk takes a cell beside it. Either way the compiler writes down what
// this block bound, so a lookup finds the innermost.
declare_name(u: *Unit, e: *Emit, name: string, at: Span, assignable: bool)
    if may_cell(e, name)
        push_n(e, DeclareSlot(claim_slot(e, name, assignable)), at, 1, 0)
        return

    note_scope_name(e, name)
    push_n(e, if assignable then DeclareVar(intern_str(u, name)) else DeclareVal(intern_str(u, name)), at, 1, 0)

// Read a name.
load_name(u: *Unit, e: *Emit, name: string, at: Span)
    slot_named(e, name) match
        Some(i) -> push_n(e, LoadSlot(e.slot_of.at(i)), at, 0, 1)
        None -> push_n(e, LoadName(intern_str(u, name)), at, 0, 1)

// Write to a name.
//
// **A write to a slot holding a `val` still emits something that faults**, rather than being refused
// here: the checker already complains about it before the machine runs, and the one path where it
// does not should say what it has always said.
store_name(u: *Unit, e: *Emit, name: string, at: Span)
    slot_named(e, name) match
        Some(i) ->
            if slot_writable(e, i)
                push_n(e, StoreSlot(e.slot_of.at(i)), at, 1, 0)
            else
                push_n(e, ImmutableSlot(intern_str(u, name)), at, 1, 0)
        None -> push_n(e, StoreName(intern_str(u, name)), at, 1, 0)

// -- a binding site that takes a pattern apart -------------------------------------------------------

// **These two are the ONLY places that decide how a PATTERN binds**, for the reason the three above
// are the only places that decide how a name does: the slots are claimed here and the instruction
// that fills them is emitted here, so a site cannot claim one thing and fill another.
//
// **THE PATTERN IS RESOLVED ONCE.** `resolve_pattern` complains about a generic type used without
// its arguments, so resolving a second time to count the names would report that twice -- and a
// report that names one mistake twice is a report a reader stops trusting.

// `val [a, b] = xs`, a `for` head, and a parameter that takes its argument apart.
//
// **The defaults belong here rather than to the caller**, because which instruction guards them is
// the same decision as which instruction binds: a chunk with cells asks its own mask and one with a
// scope asks the scope, and splitting the two across two files is how they would come to disagree.
bind_pattern(u: *Unit, e: *Emit, pat: Pattern, at: Span, assignable: bool)
    val resolved = resolve_pattern(pat, 0)
    val k = intern_pattern(u, resolved)

    if pattern_declares(e, pat)
        push_n(e, Unpack(k, !assignable), at, 1, 0)
        note_pattern_names(e, pat)
        emit_pattern_defaults(u, e, pat, !assignable)
        return
    end if

    val masked = pattern_defaults(resolved)
    val first = claim_pattern_slots(e, resolved, assignable)

    push_n(e, UnpackSlots(k, first, masked), at, 1, if masked then 1 else 0)

    if !masked then return

    emit_slot_defaults(u, e, resolved, first, at)
    push_n(e, Pop, at, 1, 0)

// One guarded assignment per defaulted name, indexed by the walk the cells were claimed with.
//
// **`pattern_names` and `pattern_default_exprs` are the pair, and `emit_pattern_defaults` may NOT be
// used here.** The two in `js_pat.sysl` mirror each other line for line and are what the cells and
// the mask are both numbered by; the one in `compile.sysl` walks the pattern its own way and does
// not visit `At`'s name, an array's rest, a variant or an `and`, so its positions are a different
// numbering that happens to agree wherever a default is legal today.
private emit_slot_defaults(u: *Unit, e: *Emit, resolved: Pattern, first: usize, at: Span)
    var names: Buf[string] = buf()
    var defaults: Buf[Option[Expr]] = buf()

    pattern_names(resolved, &names)
    pattern_default_exprs(resolved, &defaults)

    for i in 0..<names.len()
        if i >= defaults.len() then break

        defaults.at(i) match
            Some(given) ->
                val over = put(e, JumpIfSet(i, 0), given.span)

                compile_expr(u, e, given)
                push_n(e, StoreSlot(first + i), given.span, 1, 0)
                patch(e, over, here(e))
            None -> ()
end emit_slot_defaults

// A `match` arm, which reads the subject where it lies and answers whether it took.
test_pattern(u: *Unit, e: *Emit, pat: Pattern, at: Span)
    val resolved = resolve_pattern(pat, 0)
    val k = intern_pattern(u, resolved)

    if pattern_declares(e, pat)
        push_n(e, TestPat(k), at, 0, 1)
        note_pattern_names(e, pat)
        return
    end if

    push_n(e, TestSlots(k, claim_pattern_slots(e, resolved, false)), at, 0, 1)

// Whether this pattern binds a name that must live in the scope.
//
// **A BINDING SITE IS ALL OR NOTHING WHERE A BARE NAME IS PER NAME**, and the reason is the
// instruction: `UnpackSlots` fills a RUN of cells the matcher's collecting walk pushed in order, so
// a pattern one of whose names must stay in the scope has no run to fill. Such a site takes the
// scope path whole, which is what it did before any of this existed.
//
// **IT IS ONE QUESTION ASKED OF THE UNRESOLVED PATTERN, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS TWO DECISIONS IN
// STEP.** The block that opens the scope and the site that binds into it have to agree exactly -- a
// site taking the scope path inside a block that pushed none would declare into somebody else's, and
// the binding would outlive its block. So both ask this and neither works it out again.
// `pattern_binders` answers the same set `pattern_names` gathers off the RESOLVED pattern, a declared
// type not being allowed to bind, so asking before resolving costs nothing and avoids resolving twice.
//
// Not `private`: `emit.sysl` asks it where a loop head or a `match` arm opens a block.
pattern_declares(e: *Emit, pat: Pattern) -> bool
    if !e.cells then return true

    var names: Buf[string] = buf()

    pattern_binders(pat, &names)

    for i in 0..<names.len()
        if !may_cell(e, names.at(i)) then return true

    false
end pattern_declares

// Write down what a scope-bound pattern put in this block, so a lookup stops at it.
//
// **Without this an inner binding is invisible and an outer CELL of the same spelling answers for
// it.** A chunk that has cells at all can hold both kinds, and `slot_named` walks one list.
private note_pattern_names(e: *Emit, pat: Pattern)
    if !e.cells then return

    var names: Buf[string] = buf()

    pattern_binders(pat, &names)

    for i in 0..<names.len()
        note_scope_name(e, names.at(i))
end note_pattern_names

// One slot per name, in the order the machine hands their values back, answering the first.
//
// **`pattern_names` is the order, and it is the JavaScript back end's own** -- both back ends read a
// pattern's bindings out of one flat list, so reusing that walk is what keeps the two from drifting
// about which value is which. A pattern that binds nothing claims nothing and the first is not read.
private claim_pattern_slots(e: *Emit, resolved: Pattern, assignable: bool) -> usize
    var names: Buf[string] = buf()

    pattern_names(resolved, &names)

    var first: usize = 0

    for i in 0..<names.len()
        val got = claim_slot(e, names.at(i), assignable)

        if i == 0 then first = got

    first
end claim_pattern_slots

// -- what a pattern in this body binds ---------------------------------------------------------------

// Whether matching this pattern would put a name into the scope the machine is standing in.
//
// **A chunk that slots has no `EnvObj` of its own** -- `vm.scope` is the closure's captured scope --
// so a pattern that binds while such a chunk runs writes its name into a scope that OUTLIVES the
// call, and every later call sees it. That is the whole reason this exists: a test position binds
// too. `v is n` binds `n`, and a chunk holding one may not slot until the binding has somewhere of
// its own to go.
//
// **A bare name is a binding only where nothing declares a type of that name**, which is
// `resolve_pattern`'s own rule and is why `names_a_type` is asked rather than assumed. `v is Point`
// looks a class up and binds nothing; `v is n` binds.
private pattern_binds(pat: Pattern) -> bool
    *pat.kind match
        Bind(name) -> !names_a_type(name)

        // The name is the binding whatever the sub-pattern turns out to be.
        At(_, _) -> true
        ArrayPat(items, _, rest, _) ->
            if rest.is_some() then return true

            any_binds(items)
        ObjectPat(_, values, _, _) -> any_binds(values)
        VariantPat(_, items, _, _) -> any_binds(items)
        MadeFrom(_, inner) -> pattern_binds(inner)
        GenericPat(_, inner) -> pattern_binds(inner)
        AppliedPat(_, args) -> any_binds(args)
        EveryPat(_, element) -> pattern_binds(element)
        FnPat(params, result) -> any_binds(params) || pattern_binds(result)
        OrPat(alts) -> any_binds(alts)
        AndPat(parts) -> any_binds(parts)

        // The kinds that bind nothing. Written out rather than left to a wildcard for the reason
        // every other exhaustive walk here is: a kind added later must be classified rather than
        // fall through as harmless.
        Wild -> false
        TypePat(_) -> false
        TypeVarPat(_) -> false
        ProtoPat(_, _, _) -> false
        DataPat(_, _) -> false
        IntPat(_) -> false
        RealPat(_) -> false
        StrPat(_) -> false
        BoolPat(_) -> false
        NullPat -> false
end pattern_binds

// The names a pattern binds, which is `pattern_binds` again with the answers kept.
//
// **The same set `pattern_names` gathers off the RESOLVED pattern, and it can be because a declared
// type may not bind.** `type Pair = [a, b]` is refused in as many words -- *"a type is a shape, so
// `Pair` may not bind `a`"* -- so replacing a name that stands for a type can never introduce a
// binding that was not written here. That refusal is what lets this walk answer before the pattern
// is resolved, and it is the whole of why the two agree.
//
// The ORDER is not read here: `may_slot` wants the set, and the compiler claims its slots off
// `pattern_names` at the site itself.
// **It does NOT collapse a repeat, where `pattern_mentions` does**, and the difference is the point:
// this walk's COUNT has to be `pattern_names`' count, because that is how many cells a site claims
// and how many bits its mask has. A name written twice in one pattern is two cells.
//
// Not `private`: `pattern_declares` is asked from `emit.sysl`, where a block is opened.
pattern_binders(pat: Pattern, into: *Buf[string])
    *pat.kind match
        Bind(name) -> if !names_a_type(name) then into.push(name)
        At(name, inner) ->
            into.push(name)
            pattern_binders(inner, into)
        ArrayPat(items, _, rest, _) ->
            each_binder(items, into)

            rest match
                Some(name) -> into.push(name)
                None -> ()
        ObjectPat(_, values, _, _) -> each_binder(values, into)
        VariantPat(_, items, _, _) -> each_binder(items, into)
        MadeFrom(_, inner) -> pattern_binders(inner, into)
        GenericPat(_, inner) -> pattern_binders(inner, into)
        AppliedPat(_, args) -> each_binder(args, into)
        EveryPat(_, element) -> pattern_binders(element, into)
        FnPat(params, result) ->
            each_binder(params, into)
            pattern_binders(result, into)
        OrPat(alts) -> each_binder(alts, into)
        AndPat(parts) -> each_binder(parts, into)
        Wild -> ()
        TypePat(_) -> ()
        TypeVarPat(_) -> ()
        ProtoPat(_, _, _) -> ()
        DataPat(_, _) -> ()
        IntPat(_) -> ()
        RealPat(_) -> ()
        StrPat(_) -> ()
        BoolPat(_) -> ()
        NullPat -> ()
end pattern_binders

private each_binder(xs: Buf[Pattern], into: *Buf[string])
    for i in 0..<xs.len()
        pattern_binders(xs.at(i), into)

// Whether a binding site written with this pattern may take slots.
//
// **The question is whether the machine's collecting walk and `pattern_names` agree, value for
// value, in order.** The compiler claims one slot per name `pattern_names` gathers and the machine
// writes one cell per value the walk pushes; a pattern where the two counts could differ would put
// every binding after the disagreement into the wrong cell, silently. So this refuses the two shapes
// where they can:
//
// - **A DEFAULT, where the site carries no mask.** The matcher leaves such a name unbound and still
//   matches -- that is what makes a default a default -- so the walk would push nothing where
//   `pattern_names` counted one and everything after it would shift. A binding site answers that
//   with a per-name mask and a placeholder, exactly as a call does for its parameters, and passes
//   `masked`; a `match` arm has no mask and refuses defaults outright anyway.
// - **A kind `pattern_names` gathers nothing from that could still bind.** An alternative may not
//   bind, `array of T` says nothing about names, and a function type is a shape -- so where one of
//   those does bind, the two walks are already out of step and this is not the place to reconcile
//   them.
//
// **Everything else recurses**, and the kinds that bind nothing at all are written out rather than
// left to a wildcard for the reason every exhaustive walk in this file is.
private slottable_pattern(pat: Pattern, masked: bool) -> bool
    *pat.kind match
        At(_, inner) -> slottable_pattern(inner, masked)
        ArrayPat(items, _, _, defaults) ->
            if !masked && any_default(defaults) then return false

            all_slottable(items, masked)
        ObjectPat(_, values, defaults, optional) ->
            if !masked && any_default(defaults) then return false

            // **A `?` is never slottable, mask or no mask.** A default fills its name in where the
            // subject supplied nothing; a `?` says the name is simply absent, and there is nothing
            // to fill a cell with -- so a read of it would find whatever the cell held before, where
            // a scope had nothing and said so. It cannot reach a BINDING site at all
            // (`refuse_optionals`); a `match` arm is where one can be written.
            for i in 0..<optional.len()
                if optional.at(i) then return false

            all_slottable(values, masked)
        VariantPat(_, items, _, _) -> all_slottable(items, masked)
        MadeFrom(_, inner) -> slottable_pattern(inner, masked)
        AndPat(parts) -> all_slottable(parts, masked)

        // The four `pattern_names` has no arm for, allowed only while they bind nothing.
        OrPat(alts) -> !any_binds(alts)
        EveryPat(_, element) -> !pattern_binds(element)
        FnPat(params, result) -> !any_binds(params) && !pattern_binds(result)
        GenericPat(_, inner) -> !pattern_binds(inner)
        AppliedPat(_, args) -> !any_binds(args)
        Bind(_) -> true
        Wild -> true
        TypePat(_) -> true
        TypeVarPat(_) -> true
        ProtoPat(_, _, _) -> true
        DataPat(_, _) -> true
        IntPat(_) -> true
        RealPat(_) -> true
        StrPat(_) -> true
        BoolPat(_) -> true
        NullPat -> true
end slottable_pattern

private all_slottable(xs: Buf[Pattern], masked: bool) -> bool
    for i in 0..<xs.len()
        if !slottable_pattern(xs.at(i), masked) then return false

    true

// A binding site's pattern: slottable, within the mask's width, and its two lists of names taken.
//
// **The width is counted here rather than left to `slottable_pattern`**, which asks about one node
// at a time and cannot see a total -- and a mask shifted by 64 is not a mask.
private bindable_here(pat: Pattern, mine: *Buf[string], kept: *Buf[string]) -> bool
    if !slottable_pattern(pat, true) then return false

    val before = mine.len()

    pattern_binders(pat, mine)

    if mine.len() - before > MaskBits then return false

    pattern_mentions(pat, kept)
    true
end bindable_here

private any_default(defaults: Buf[Option[Expr]]) -> bool
    for i in 0..<defaults.len()
        if defaults.at(i).is_some() then return true

    false

private any_binds(xs: Buf[Pattern]) -> bool
    for i in 0..<xs.len()
        if pattern_binds(xs.at(i)) then return true

    false

// -- what a pattern in this body looks up -----------------------------------------------------------

// Every bare name a pattern mentions, wherever it mentions one.
//
// **A name in pattern position is resolved to a TYPE while compiling, or bound while matching, and
// this cannot tell which** -- the declared types are not in view here. So it collects both, and
// `may_slot` refuses a chunk that would slot any of them. A name that turns out to have been an
// ordinary binding costs that chunk its slots and nothing else; a name that turns out to have been a
// class and was let through costs an `is` its answer.
//
// **`ProtoPat`, `DataPat`, `VariantPat`, `MadeFrom` and `AppliedPat` are in the list even though a
// body's pattern has not been resolved yet.** They are what a resolved one becomes, and a walk that
// is exhaustive over the kinds is a walk that stops compiling when a kind is added -- which is the
// only reason any of this can be trusted.
private pattern_mentions(pat: Pattern, into: *Buf[string])
    *pat.kind match
        // **A bare name is looked up only where it names a TYPE**, which is `resolve_pattern`'s own
        // rule -- so `v is Point` is a lookup and `v is n` is a binding, and only the first belongs
        // here. Asking `names_a_type` rather than assuming the worst is what lets a `match` arm on a
        // class stand in a chunk that slots.
        Bind(name) -> if names_a_type(name) then note_mention(name, into)

        // `p @ Point` names the binding on the left and looks up whatever the sub-pattern names.
        At(_, inner) -> pattern_mentions(inner, into)
        TypeVarPat(name) -> note_mention(name, into)
        AppliedPat(name, args) ->
            note_mention(name, into)
            each_mention(args, into)
        ProtoPat(name, _, _) -> note_mention(name, into)
        DataPat(name, _) -> note_mention(name, into)
        VariantPat(name, items, _, _) ->
            note_mention(name, into)
            each_mention(items, into)
        MadeFrom(name, inner) ->
            note_mention(name, into)
            pattern_mentions(inner, into)
        GenericPat(params, inner) ->
            for i in 0..<params.len()
                note_mention(params.at(i), into)

            pattern_mentions(inner, into)
        // **The tail's name is a BINDING and never a lookup**, so it belongs in the other list.
        // Noting it here made every `[head, ...rest]` collide with itself: the name went into the
        // chunk's own names and into the names its patterns look up, and the overlap check refused
        // the chunk over a name that was only ever written once.
        ArrayPat(items, _, _, _) -> each_mention(items, into)
        ObjectPat(_, values, _, _) -> each_mention(values, into)
        EveryPat(_, element) -> pattern_mentions(element, into)
        FnPat(params, result) ->
            each_mention(params, into)
            pattern_mentions(result, into)
        OrPat(alts) -> each_mention(alts, into)
        AndPat(parts) -> each_mention(parts, into)

        // The kinds that name nothing. Written out for the reason the expression walk's literals
        // are: a kind added later should stop this compiling rather than fall through as harmless.
        Wild -> ()
        TypePat(_) -> ()
        IntPat(_) -> ()
        RealPat(_) -> ()
        StrPat(_) -> ()
        BoolPat(_) -> ()
        NullPat -> ()
end pattern_mentions

private each_mention(xs: Buf[Pattern], into: *Buf[string])
    for i in 0..<xs.len()
        pattern_mentions(xs.at(i), into)

private note_mention(name: string, into: *Buf[string])
    for i in 0..<into.len()
        if into.at(i) == name then return

    into.push(name)
